        <div class="post-hero-inner">
            <h1>The US CLOUD Act and the Illusion of Data Sovereignty</h1>
            <time datetime="2026-04-17">17.04.2026</time>
        </div>
    </header>
    <div class="post-body">
        <p>The Clarifying Lawful Overseas Use of Data Act — the CLOUD Act — was signed into law in 2018. It does something simple: it gives US law enforcement the legal authority to compel any US-based technology company to hand over data stored on its servers, regardless of where in the world those servers are physically located.</p>
<p>If your organization uses Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, AWS, or any US-headquartered cloud provider, the CLOUD Act applies to your data. It does not matter that your office is in Berlin, that your servers are in Frankfurt, or that your contract specifies EU data residency. The legal jurisdiction follows the company, not the data center.</p>
<p>This is not a theoretical risk.</p>
<figure>
  <picture>
